8344/assets/885f9b9ea95909edf81c756397bff73ca8a83619117f51c9db7433c5082cf354.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=h(x)$ above. What is the point of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "The points of inflection are the points where\n\n- the function's concavity changes sign, and\n\n- the tangent line to the graph exists.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our graph.\n\n- The point $x=-1$ is a point of inflection. Indeed:\n\n- the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x=-1,$ and\n\n- the tangent line exists at this point.\n\n- The point $x=0$ is *not* a point of inflection for two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave up both to the left and to the right of $x=0.$ Second, the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\n- The point $x=1$ is *not* a point of inflection. Although the graph changes concavity from concave up to concave down at $x=1,$   the function is not defined at this point, so the tangent line does not exist either.\n\nTherefore, the point of inflection is $x=-1$ only." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-127112/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0099e032-f2ee-573a-a359-cf60e28bc89e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-127112"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-51423/selection/565f087efc172cc689d6ac7a38ae1818e245a6f8995ecba68bcdf0ac5950b2ea" :answer/id #uuid "eb45b26b-5768-57de-97fe-6add6959626a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-51423/selection/517b13fa7d7411493417798333c215e538be20515774e23158b4c71536604686" :answer/id #uuid "b0da4bd6-15f6-5ded-ad62-6714c3255db7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-51423/selection/9f75d63bbce36d71b1687120236f1905d3381f1a10419d07fe4d39d3b5a3b550" :answer/id #uuid "bec13d20-7547-5fcb-a8a2-62591c09858b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/