oncave up both to the left and to the right of $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, there are no points of inflection." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-114404/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0099e032-f2ee-573a-a359-cf60e28bc89e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-114404"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-51428"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/assets/a8fadcc272d96492194b2d46f1ae66151fb4fd16b2ee42a6840ff335aa0b4262.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. What are the points of inflection?" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-114402/selection/517b13fa7d7411493417798333c215e538be20515774e23158b4c71536604686" :answer/id #uuid "d1bdf0f5-b3fe-5471-a098-cea3d66ac947" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114402/selection/82d8beec48d8e7000728fc93bb99e8896041037eb2d23f8dc73b025ed9a4e82c" :answer/id #uuid "f7f384a9-2f74-52c8-882c-570343ba1435" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=3$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114402/selection/33d372f945408087319715af47b73ac3c8a80d426ba7875b9a00d4ab71b4ab14" :answer/id #uuid "41aa9c73-5af6-5b30-be41-696e1204b901" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=1$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114402/selection/8a2b18b7b53bd4ed2ff985eb646cdcb58b10965c01b5903461d171147b4d90b4" :answer/id #uuid "4b780da8-cbf8-5cb3-b18c-a90e9b11bf9f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-2,$ $x=1,$ and $x=3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114402/selection/aa5f88109c2898796852405a10976e80a9533cff52f5ec7f371f531fbec900d0" :answer/id #uuid "c7ade8a1-544b-55c3-aba7-3741f93d9a33" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=3$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114402/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a45ea804-c24e-5861-a32d-d80d7817241b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-114402/selection/8a2b18b7b53bd4ed2ff985eb646cdcb58b10965c01b5903461d171147b4d90b4" :answer-field/choices ["q-114402/selection/517b13fa7d7411493417798333c215e538be20515774e23158b4c71536604686" "q-114402/selection/82d8beec48d8e7000728fc93bb99e8896041037eb2d23f8dc73b025ed9a4e82c" "q-114402/selection/33d372f945408087319715af47b73ac3c8a80d426ba7875b9a00d4ab71b4ab14" "q-114402/selection/8a2b18b7b53bd4ed2ff985eb646cdcb58b10965c01b5903461d171147b4d90b4" "q-114402/selection/aa5f88109c2898796852405a10976e80a9533cff52f5ec7f371f531fbec900d0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-114402" :question/id #uuid "444031a3-3309-59e7-a714-c81365022d27" :question/math-academy-id "q-114402" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/assets/20b6532fc5d99c74a26bd752e19aa2a6451ea26a79d2a129e4c0203bc9133354.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x$ in $(-4,4).$ The graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x=-2,$ $x=1,$ and $x=3.$ \n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=-2,$ $x=1,$ and $x=3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-114402/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "977a9b6e-