estion/math-academy-id "q-114416" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/assets/826336bca267300c9b98e43072f74bb5efdacff0a4007fb7b938eb61893d8fbe.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x$ in $(-4,4).$ The graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x=2.$ \n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has point of inflection at $x=2$ only." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-114416/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "977a9b6e-744a-554b-92ee-0b6343041ecf"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-114416"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-2753/selection/0d782dc17ff43c02f50a0e3c54713c0f768ac9dfff69730a158d17df7241cb36" :answer/id #uuid "78c103ee-6059-5303-a35f-46554447d0e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-2753/selection/84ea79387d51fde1e84146322c8639e6e5eae9a28be16c7c74f23a3fe78716de" :answer/id #uuid "3a1ed625-4341-58b3-aa10-33f47944f557" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "There are no points of inflection" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-2753/selection/c143ca64ec013e5fe4018485f8c456b26425ea6baf2691e509470a46cd90ac4d" :answer/id #uuid "34e09784-599c-5cd4-bb0e-ff216bc290e6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-2753/selection/c1f9d865dec6d479032191d60cec3d55c280839e6f35d6c3721277787c0e26f1" :answer/id #uuid "383c080f-9929-598b-93c7-36281679d275" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-2753/selection/9a4c654c26e0048811684292989d39180dbcf802b2aff4eb6076cc53d4d8f320" :answer/id #uuid "d5edd98b-8127-5e02-855e-df6e05d9c4c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-2753/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "30be49a0-1ad1-529c-833f-cacee5950bd1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-2753/selection/9a4c654c26e0048811684292989d39180dbcf802b2aff4eb6076cc53d4d8f320" :answer-field/choices ["q-2753/selection/0d782dc17ff43c02f50a0e3c54713c0f768ac9dfff69730a158d17df7241cb36" "q-2753/selection/84ea79387d51fde1e84146322c8639e6e5eae9a28be16c7c74f23a3fe78716de" "q-2753/selection/c143ca64ec013e5fe4018485f8c456b26425ea6baf2691e509470a46cd90ac4d" "q-2753/selection/c1f9d865dec6d479032191d60cec3d55c280839e6f35d6c3721277787c0e26f1" "q-2753/selection/9a4c654c26e0048811684292989d39180dbcf802b2aff4eb6076cc53d4d8f320"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-2753" :question/id #uuid "82843c7f-f0f2-5349-b0a0-01241922fac4" :question/math-academy-id "q-2753" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ on the interval $(0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0$ on the interval $(0,2\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\pi \n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check if the concavity changes at $x=\\pi .$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-1 \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=\\pi ,$ we conclude that $x=\\pi $ is indeed a point of inflection." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-2753/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "841151db-1c26-5059-9796-75b70e41f659"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-2753"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-344063/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "88841588-0fb1-5108-9559-255db6413849" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186294039 :answer-field/choices ["q-344063/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-21047/selection/592b9f18cf04a8c75d513e20b1b4a536540e9c3c0f5c7614ffaf1943dc6c7d15" :answer/id #uuid "a9205c82-6fd0-5c53-95e6-38718a4f2164" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-21047/selection/9793f24194f8da660192c2af9cab517906709f80104ac625cb96a9d7b6888bcb" :answer/id #uuid "79fc881a-1905-50fa-a4e8-3d4d0c9c011e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-21047/selection/da6ec38f87287c48f97b7f5538a006b7adbc63bec0be5e152accc6b9f3071e1b" :answer/id #uuid "ee95f8d3-6d03-5a52-9899-381aeba3d399" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-21047/selection/cb1896197a67f182a612a87dbfdf77a8994e499a7df0036f929b0112dee816e0" :answer/id #uuid "ec7c21d3-47fc-59b0-9678-4719bc0e838e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-21047/selection/2f080de0ef65f6e5dd2806fa211066253fb2f37c1d406be458ab4efcf0eceed2" :answer/id #uuid "fba25312-794c-546b-8978-1447f36282ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-21047/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "898e8b2c-817b-5b6d-b909-be519e9eb032" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-21047/selection/2f080de0ef65f6e5dd2806fa211066253fb2f37c1d406be458ab4efcf0eceed2" :answer-field/choices ["q-21047/selection/592b9f18cf04a8c75d513e20b1b4a536540e9c3c0f5c7614ffaf1943dc6c7d15" "q-21047/selection/9793f24194f8da660192c2af9cab517906709f80104ac625cb96a9d7b6888bcb" "q-21047/selection/da6ec38f87287c48f97b7f5538a006b7adbc63bec0be5e152accc6b9f3071e1b" "q-21047/selection/cb1896197a67f182a612a87dbfdf77a8994e499a7df0036f929b0112dee816e0" "q-21047/selection/2f080de0ef65f6e5dd2806fa211066253fb2f37c1d406be458ab4efcf0eceed2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-21047" :question/id #uuid "43a049b9-92e8-5818-8daf-281bf457ff15" :question/math-academy-id "q-21047" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=8{x}^{2}-3x-\\frac{1}{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, notice that $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=0,$ so this can't be a point of inflection.\n\nNow, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =16x-3+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =16-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & =16 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ and $x=1$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{4}) & =16-\\frac{2}{{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3}}=-112<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =16-\\frac{2}{(1)^{3}}=14>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ we conclude that $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ is indeed a point of inflection." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-21047/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "841151db-1c26-5059-9796-75b70e41f659"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-21047"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-2460/selection/ce39085a965090326eeacdad7999bb54bd417e299e83a73a7e22c1a2dd9b8f43" :answer/id #uuid "fbba851c-96e0-5a8a-a6a2-7cc331323305" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "No inflection point exists" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-2460/selection/4507af753e94c6f82db929d331e6749b5de730f7a1cfa515ef7e9eae88a0fbc8" :answer/id #uuid "477f16b0-d9d3-5bc6-aa65-b18f291ff324" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-2460/selection/6bac05a62229f29eba4498544257eb386a58da0ea736d3d95ca346c883c43950" :answer/id #uuid "5a4b95a5-b48b-50f9-a4de-87c5be2b29ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-2460/selection/2457768122718de57e77bf281f345e9b3101ba474f1bfe0b3b935c89934fe6e6" :answer/id #uuid "ca8858be-4fe4-5ebf-be03-cb245cef263a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-2460/selection/4e6eeef5aa33ee4a1d2fff0c2887be5f32fe5006030d9cd9500bd4b039270d64" :answer/id #uuid "12d78119-1241-5d3b-9cea-1301412362fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-2460/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "4be8ad13-3a15-5bec-88b9-a5aa40d7a154" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-2460/selection/6bac05a62229f29eba4498544257eb386a58da0ea736d3d95ca346c883c43950" :answer-field/choices ["q-2460/selection/ce39085a965090326eeacdad7999bb54bd417e299e83a73a7e22c1a2dd9b8f43" "q-2460/selection/4507af753e94c6f82db929d331e6749b5de730f7a1cfa515ef7e9eae88a0fbc8" "q-2460/selection/6bac05a62229f29eba4498544257eb386a58da0ea736d3d95ca346c883c43950" "q-2460/selection/2457768122718de57e77bf281f345e9b3101ba474f1bfe0b3b935c89934fe6e6" "q-2460/selection/4e6eeef5aa33ee4a1d2fff0c2887be5f32fe5006030d9cd9500bd4b039270d64"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-2460" :question/id #uuid "137cf379-31e0-5aef-a23a-29d963a5bad4" :question/math-academy-id "q-2460" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)={x}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n6x=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check if the concavity changes at $x=0.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=-1$ and $x=1$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1) & =6(-1)=-6<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =6(1)=6>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Therefore, since the concavity changes at $x=0,$ we conclude that $x=0$ is indeed a point of inflection." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-2460/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "841151db-1c26-5059-9796-75b70e41f659"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-2