944-a4fd-b2809780da32" :question/math-academy-id "q-2465" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-6x+7.$" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =6{x}^{2}+12x-6 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =12x+12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n12x+12=0\\,⟹\\,x=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check if the concavity changes at $x=-1.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=-2$ and $x=0$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-2) & =12(-2)+12=-12<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(0) & =12(0)+12=12>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=-1,$ we conclude that $x=-1$ is indeed a point of inflection." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-2465/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "841151db-1c26-5059-9796-75b70e41f659"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-2465"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-114413/selection/6bac05a62229f29eba4498544257eb386a58da0ea736d3d95ca346c883c43950" :answer/id #uuid "7b123f5f-7687-556d-bb81-fd29f6f16416" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114413/selection/84ea79387d51fde1e84146322c8639e6e5eae9a28be16c7c74f23a3fe78716de" :answer/id #uuid "4662a91e-0f0c-514d-ab7d-d9d9d14d1bdd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "There are no points of inflection" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114413/selection/3bf490a97b4b01d003a4d517d1cd8f80a0e11ffcaac2332bbdf3cd4e7da1ce42" :answer/id #uuid "1100cc7f-aaa6-55ff-b1c7-2017629861cf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114413/selection/02c2ac9730c208f517b45425f4e43eb110953149e4da3e6c03d6ca5b06cd40f6" :answer/id #uuid "0f8dcdd9-07a4-5751-b163-141ec66cb107" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114413/selection/0bf5abed43f6f20dd466b36279de8736a04c540ff5306cb048ae22803a7b3f9c" :answer/id #uuid "f35f1df9-77b9-5fe4-bb1a-511f3b9d2549" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114413/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5efe734d-5228-5332-aacd-08464c673489" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-114413/selection/84ea79387d51fde1e84146322c8639e6e5eae9a28be16c7c74f23a3fe78716de" :answer-field/choices ["q-114413/selection/6bac05a62229f29eba4498544257eb386a58da0ea736d3d95ca346c883c43950" "q-114413/selection/84ea79387d51fde1e84146322c8639e6e5eae9a28be16c7c74f23a3fe78716de" "q-114413/selection/3bf490a97b4b01d003a4d517d1cd8f80a0e11ffcaac2332bbdf3cd4e7da1ce42" "q-114413/selection/02c2ac9730c208f517b45425f4e43eb110953149e4da3e6c03d6ca5b06cd40f6" "q-114413/selection/0bf5abed43f6f20dd466b36279de8736a04c540ff5306cb048ae22803a7b3f9c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-114413" :question/id #uuid "5145ec8c-5097-582c-b02c-15f0c24181cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-114413" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-4x+7).$" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-4x+7)+{e}^{x}(2x-4) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-2x+3) \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-2x+3)+{e}^{x}(2x-2) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{2}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$\n\nSince ${e}^{x}({x}^{2}+1)>0$ for all real values of $x,$ the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0$ has no real solutions, and so there are no points of inflection." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-114413/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a363f215-ed40-504a-bcae-620b7d585678"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-114413"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-344206/field-1/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "2992ee29-95d1-58dc-a11b-42f2c03ce2c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344206/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "fd655f84-7eed-549b-8f62-60b2c9c27764" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344206/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "86e3b102-6275-5f93-ab85-bc58a01f6d2d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344206/field-1/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer-field/choices ["q-344206/field-1/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" "q-344206/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344206" :question/id #uuid "9830b87c-ec53-5f59-89c6-b1659da4f4ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-344206" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinates of all points of inflection for $g(x)=3{x}^{5}+15{x}^{4}-2x+5.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${g}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =15{x}^{4}+60{x}^{3}-2 \\\\ {g}^{″}(x) & =60{x}^{3}+180{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60{x}^{3}+180{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 60{x}^{2}(x+3) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=-3$ and $x=0.$ So we choose points around them, say, \n\n$\\,$ $x=-4,\\,$ $x=-1,\\,$ and $x=1,$\n\n and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(-4) & =60(-4)^{3}+180(-4)^{2}=-960<0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(-1) & =60(-1)^{3}+180(-1)^{2}=120>0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(1) & =60(1)^{3}+180(1)^{2}=240>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- The second derivative changes sign at $x=-3.$ Therefore, since the concavity changes at $x=-3,$ we conclude that $x=-3$ is indeed a point of inflection.\n\n- The second derivative does not change sign at $x=0.$ Therefore, since the concavity does *not* change at $x=0,$ we conclude that $x=0$ is *not* a point of inflection.\n\nTherefore, only  $x=-3$ is a point of inflection." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-344206/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a363f215-ed40-504a-bcae-620b7d585678"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-344206"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-344246/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "722f578c-86db-52bc-9c57-5e83bbf92be2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186294054 :answer-field/choices ["q-344246/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-20850"] :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of all points of inflection for $g(x)={x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-2x.$" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =4{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}+12x-2 \\\\ {g}^{″}(x) & =12{x}^{2}-24x+12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{x}^{2}-24x+12 & =0 \\\\ 12(x-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=1.$ So we pick two points around it, say, \n\n$\\,$ $x=0\\,$ and $\\,x=2,$ \n\nand compute the sign of ${g}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(0) & =12(0-1)^{2}=12>0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(2) & =12(2-1)^{2}=12>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity does *not* change at $x=1,$ we conclude that $x=1$ is *not* a point of inflection." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3739"] :question/problem "Consider the function $y = f(x)$ below, defined on $x\\in(-2,8).$ At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/assets/54d2fe17d8d36a23c7c714242ffbfa12a56fd8e870b7672f1ce9b7e02f6508b4.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3742"] :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y = f''(x)$ is shown below. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/assets/6b146f4a7bec69f1e984b3e2aa0e3c38ea65c4464da037759b41eac2fd97325f.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
